# Build output
/target
**/target

# mdBook output (source lives in book/src; build with `mdbook build` in book/)
/book/book

# Cargo.lock *is* committed, and it changes nothing for consumers: cargo ignores
# a dependency's lockfile, so every downstream application still resolves its
# own versions. What it buys is CI testing the dependency set the author tested,
# rather than a second one. Without it CI re-resolved to the newest crates on
# every run, and hyper 1.11 — which
# strips a request's Content-Length when a Transfer-Encoding is present — turned
# a green branch red with no commit behind it. Upstream drift is still tested,
# by ci-float.yml on a schedule, where it is the subject rather than the
# ambush.

# rustc / rustfmt leftovers
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

